 ## Overview
-Schwarze's Analytic Inversion is a method for computing the exact intersection of a ray with an iso-surface defined by trilinearly interpolated scalar data within a voxel. It solves the cubic polynomial resulting from substituting the ray equation into the trilinear interpolation function to find the precise root [S1, S2]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like Neubauer's algorithm by its reliance on closed-form algebraic solutions (Cardano's formula) rather than iterative refinement or isolation techniques, offering mathematical exactness at the cost of significant computational expense and numerical instability [S1, S2].
